A||N||16||bcwawright||Fuel Tank Relocation||||5||
Z||000000||bcwawright||06-03-2001||12:37 PM||||Has anyone done or heard of someone relocating the fuel tank or installing a fuel cell in another area so that the tunnel can be removed to make the interior floor area almost flat?<P>Thanks<BR>Bruce<BR>88GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||btoth||06-03-2001||01:48 PM||||What, you don't like the center console?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  That's the best part of the car! j/k.  Try a search... I remember a year or so ago someone putting a fuel cell up front.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bcwawright||06-03-2001||02:06 PM||||I am not getting rid of the console I have a F355 rebody and need to lower the tunnel so that the new console will fit and be at the right height.<P>Bruce <BR>88GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||06-03-2001||02:29 PM||||We tossed this topic around about a month ago. It was fun! We established that it would be difficult to regain the strength lost with that tunnel structure. You would have to use heavy gauge steel to maintain the same strength lost with the tunnel. If you want to modify the tunnel solely for interior purposes, I would refrain. Now if someone wanted to put a front engine in a Fiero to be different, then it would be neccessary.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fyerstarter||06-03-2001||05:27 PM||||If you relocate the fuel tank to the front or back you probably want to build a nice protective cage around it in case of a collision. You certainly don't want gas spilling on a hot engine or radiator. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||gabriel||06-04-2001||12:42 PM||||<BR>&gt;We tossed this topic around about a month &gt;ago. It was fun! We established that it &gt;would be difficult to regain the strength &gt;lost with that tunnel structure. <P>  Fun, yes.  About 2 million stupid responses ...  Informative?  Well, lets move on.<BR>  I have removed the tunnel, both in the TR project & the 355.  In the 355 you might consider the possiblity of twin aluminum gas tanks just behind the seat area as in the real deal.  There is room with the stretch & firewall mods.  There is no need to construct any cage around them.  A rubber fuel bladder inside will do for those paraniod types. After all if the car is crushed to the seats, you are already dead ...  However, the spare tire area is also used in many conversions.<P>&gt;You would have to use heavy gauge steel to &gt;maintain the same strength lost with the &gt;tunnel. <P>  There is a typical reinforcing pattern of steel tubing to fit in this area.  Also the rocker panels are reinforced.  Together, this makes a stronger frame than the Fiero had to begin with.<P> If you want more info for the 355 project, just email me. -g||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||06-04-2001||07:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gabriel:<BR><B><BR>&gt;We tossed this topic around about a month &gt;ago. It was fun! We established that it &gt;would be difficult to regain the strength &gt;lost with that tunnel structure. <P>  Fun, yes.  About 2 million stupid responses ...  Informative?  Well, lets move on.<BR>  I have removed the tunnel, both in the TR project & the 355.  In the 355 you might consider the possiblity of twin aluminum gas tanks just behind the seat area as in the real deal.  There is room with the stretch & firewall mods.  There is no need to construct any cage around them.  A rubber fuel bladder inside will do for those paraniod types. After all if the car is crushed to the seats, you are already dead ...  However, the spare tire area is also used in many conversions.<P>&gt;You would have to use heavy gauge steel to &gt;maintain the same strength lost with the &gt;tunnel. <P>  There is a typical reinforcing pattern of steel tubing to fit in this area.  Also the rocker panels are reinforced.  Together, this makes a stronger frame than the Fiero had to begin with.<P> If you want more info for the 355 project, just email me. -g</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And you wonder why you get so called "stupid responses?" As you recall, when the issue was first introduced a month ago, the original poster didn't qualify a reason, so the respondents just figured the aformentioned just wanted to be different or create more cockpit room. When you (or whoever) qualified the post with a reason, you received many good responses. If you're as sarchastic as;<P>"There is no need to construct any cage around them.  A rubber fuel bladder inside will do for those paraniod types. After all if the car is crushed to the seats, you are already dead."<P>Then you can expect nothing positive in return. Do you really think a rubber bladder would offer as much protection as the original tank surrounded by the tranny tunnel? What if the engine gets moved around violently in the act of a crash and penetrates the tank? I'm sure you've done at least 15 minutes of crash survivability testing, compared to GM's 100's of hours. Remember, they have liability and assets to attach, you have nothing of any real value.<P>And then this;<P>"There is a typical reinforcing pattern of steel tubing to fit in this area.  Also the rocker panels are reinforced.  Together, this makes a stronger frame than the Fiero had to begin with."<P>How do you know it makes a stronger frame, because it looks like it should, or because you want it to? Besides, strength is not the only consideration. The method in which the chasis/body crumples is probably more important. You know what they say; want in one hand, sh!t in the other, see which fills up first. You claim to want info, help, advice, and opinions from the forum, but then you throw out half-cocked questions and wonder why people parody them. Take a ridalin.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||06-04-2001||08:55 PM||||I think you should take some prozac instead.||||reg||1
Z||000008||EdsB52||06-04-2001||11:38 PM||||I think you should blow yourself, Will.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||06-04-2001||11:45 PM||||If you get a race fuel cell (relatively cheap) you can have it custom made , no need for some cage around it , they have a nylon bladder. I can see the point of this for a ferrari converison , But handling wise a mid mounted gas tank is a huge asset for steady state cornering (off ramps)<P>I have seen lots of Fiero racers relocate the tank to the front , easy way to get rid of understeer. They just remove the tunnel and make a wishbone center like a lotus elan or europa (not sure if esprit is the same setup)<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000010||bcwawright||06-05-2001||12:07 AM||||Thanks Jonathan and to all that gave me some input.........<P>Bruce<BR>88GT||||reg||7
Z||000011||gabriel||06-05-2001||11:02 AM||||&gt;Then you can expect nothing positive in return. Do you really think a rubber bladder would offer as much protection as the original tank surrounded by the tranny tunnel? What if the engine gets moved around violently in the act of a crash and penetrates the tank? I'm sure you've done at least 15 minutes of crash survivability testing, compared to GM's 100's of hours. Remember, they have liability and assets to attach, you have nothing of any real value.<P>   Yes Eds, I'm sure that Ferrari & other high end autos have it all wrong. After all, they use that same mid mounted setup, but you must be correct, right?<P>&gt;How do you know it makes a stronger frame, because it looks like it should, or because you want it to? <P>  Sigh ...  Eds, it's been done on a number of conversions by a number of different people, for racing and other reasons.  Like, do you even know how to weld?<BR>Have you done any enginnering study?<BR>  A heavier gauge set of tubing, properly laid out & TIG welded is WAY stronger than the Fiero.  BTW, I work with metal every day.<P>  All that to the side, I'm not even remotely interested in getting into a flame war with you.  I'm just answering a post, and as for my opinion of some of the responses to my other post, well that was, and still is, my opinion. If that somehow pushed your buttons, well ... my apoligies. Take a breath, Eds.<p>[This message has been edited by gabriel (edited 06-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||bcwawright||06-05-2001||11:58 AM||||I appreciate everyones comments both pro and con......you can discuss a topic without insulting or degrading individuals. Anytime you cut a structural portion of a frame or monocoque chasis you weaken the structural integrity and it is possible to rebrace and reinforce to make it as strong if not stonger than the original design. It is my opinion that all fuel containers not matter what type vehicle should have a rubberized fabric bladder inlosed in a container(either aluminum, steel, or carbon/kevlar) and the reason it is not normally standard in most autos is due to price. It is possible to modify and customize a vehicle without compromising safety. I just wanted to know if someone had engineered a method to relocate the fuel container for a Fiero that was safe and effective. Most of the Fiero owners I know are very innovative and extremely knowledgeable about their vehicles. I am still open for any and all comments and suggestions without the insults.<P>Bruce <BR>88GT ||||reg||7
Z||000013||EdsB52||06-05-2001||01:16 PM||||<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gabriel:<BR><B>&gt;Then you can expect nothing positive in return. Do you really think a rubber bladder would offer as much protection as the original tank surrounded by the tranny tunnel? What if the engine gets moved around violently in the act of a crash and penetrates the tank? I'm sure you've done at least 15 minutes of crash survivability testing, compared to GM's 100's of hours. Remember, they have liability and assets to attach, you have nothing of any real value.<P>   Yes Eds, I'm sure that Ferrari & other high end autos have it all wrong. After all, they use that same mid mounted setup, but you must be correct, right?<P>&gt;How do you know it makes a stronger frame, because it looks like it should, or because you want it to? <P>  Sigh ...  Eds, it's been done on a number of conversions by a number of different people, for racing and other reasons.  Like, do you even know how to weld?<BR>Have you done any enginnering study?<BR>  A heavier gauge set of tubing, properly laid out & TIG welded is WAY stronger than the Fiero.  BTW, I work with metal every day.<P>  All that to the side, I'm not even remotely interested in getting into a flame war with you.  I'm just answering a post, and as for my opinion of some of the responses to my other post, well that was, and still is, my opinion. If that somehow pushed your buttons, well ... my apoligies. Take a breath, Eds.<P>[This message has been edited by gabriel (edited 06-05-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Gabriel wrote:<P>"Yes Eds, I'm sure that Ferrari & other high end autos have it all wrong. After all, they use that same mid mounted setup, but you must be correct, right?"<P>I'm not saying I'm right, just offering that you might not be correct with your quick assessment. Just because a structure is like another doesn't mean it is equal. Aluminum fabricated Aircraft are the epitome of design engineering when it comes to "least amount of metal for most amount of strength." Many small general aviation aircraft use a monocoque design, whereas airline aircraft use a semi-monocoque design. The former is like a pop can, with no internal structure and strength comes from the skin. The latter is what larger military or commercial aircraft use and that's with the skin incorporating sub structure comprising of stringers and frames for additional support. After these structures are designed and built, they emulate structures in existence today, but sometimes fail stress tests. So just because a structure or application looks like another doesn't guarantee equal strength or comparable collapsibility if compacted. How many hours of R&D do you think Ferrari does? How many high-end autos get totaled in the process of discovery?<P>You then wrote:<P>"Sigh ...  Eds, it's been done on a number of conversions by a number of different people, for racing and other reasons.  Like, do you even know how to weld?<BR>Have you done any enginnering study?<BR>  A heavier gauge set of tubing, properly laid out & TIG welded is WAY stronger than the Fiero.  BTW, I work with metal every day."<P><BR>Like, no, I don't know how to weld very well. Does that eliminate me from being able to argue a subject of design, strength, engineering, and probability of catastrophe in a collision? Actually, neither one of us are ultimately qualified to argue this topic, as neither of us are engineers. How many blue-collar guys do you know of that do anything else than just copy other designs and say, "it looks like that one, it should be fine." My dad has been a life-long welder who has built from plans a T-craft with 4130 steel tubing. I'm more of an aluminum guy myself and am going to build an RV-4 or 8 one day. OK, for the sake of argument, lets say you're right and your structure is the toughest thing since Mike Tyson. You get in a crash, the car crumples, and everything is smashed except the fuel tank and surrounding structure. Have you won, in an engineering sort of way? Isn't the idea to build a stronger compartment to protect the occupants? With the Fiero design, they kill two birds with one stone by having the tank in the tranny hump. If you put the tank outside that region, now you have to allow for more absorption of impact, while allowing less crumple for equal survivability results. That's just my humble opinion; maybe I'm way off base (sarcasm). By the way, working with metal and being an engineer are worlds apart. I've been an aircraft mechanic and worked with aluminum for twenty years. I'm an FAA licensed pilot, mechanic, and inspector. Does that qualify me to engineer a structure? NO. Those are called DAR's (Designated Alteration Representatives).<P>Then finally:<P>"All that to the side, I'm not even remotely interested in getting into a flame war with you.  I'm just answering a post, and as for my opinion of some of the responses to my other post, well that was, and still is, my opinion. If that somehow pushed your buttons, well ... my apoligies. Take a breath, Eds."<P>Really, it sure seems that way. Actually I'm not either. To say you're just answering a post undermines the sarcastic content and nature of your posts. You were writing as much smack as I was. No, you can't push my buttons. You don't know which buttons are mine, how could you know. Don't give yourself so much credit. I love this forum, but moreover, I love constructive arguments about various topics. You have some to offer, but you essentially just want to go out and do your project regardless of the strength involved. Cool, I like that attitude. Too much engineering and not enough fnck-it is no fun. I'm a "seat-of-my-pants" type of guy; I like to skydive, fly, scuba dive, race road-bikes, and all that stuff. You can plan, and plan, and plan until you're green, but you eventually have to say fnck-it and go out and do it. So I'm not saying be careful and don't get hurt, I'm merely saying what you're doing is guessing and maybe preliminarily saying fnck-it, and disguising that attitude with the fact that you work with metal everyday. So what. The lawyer wanna-be in me says that you should never sell your car, unless you don't mind being sued and who knows, possibly jailed for an erroneous design that could harm others. How many streetcars use remote bladders? Would a jury convict you of negligent homicide if you sold your car, had someone else wreck it and kill a minivan full of kids? By today’s standard, maybe. GM's and Ford's company executives can be exonerated for idiotic fuel tank designs, but not you or I my friend. I'm going into Law School soon, I watch many high-profile trials, and am a Justice Major at ASU, am I qualified to say that?  <P>I think one of the original arguments that we have gotten away from is the one of why. I think you wanted to do this so your interior would fit better. Is it worth it?<P>Then you end your post with "take a breath" following an apology. How disingenuous.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Will||06-05-2001||01:39 PM||||I was poking around under my car last weekend (and the weekend before, and...).<BR>There seems to be a void in the chassis directly in front of the gas tank. If you cut into that space, It might be useable for another 1/2 - 1 gallon of fuel.||||reg||1
Z||000015||EdsB52||06-05-2001||02:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bcwawright:<BR><B>I appreciate everyones comments both pro and con......you can discuss a topic without insulting or degrading individuals. Anytime you cut a structural portion of a frame or monocoque chasis you weaken the structural integrity and it is possible to rebrace and reinforce to make it as strong if not stonger than the original design. It is my opinion that all fuel containers not matter what type vehicle should have a rubberized fabric bladder inlosed in a container(either aluminum, steel, or carbon/kevlar) and the reason it is not normally standard in most autos is due to price. It is possible to modify and customize a vehicle without compromising safety. I just wanted to know if someone had engineered a method to relocate the fuel container for a Fiero that was safe and effective. Most of the Fiero owners I know are very innovative and extremely knowledgeable about their vehicles. I am still open for any and all comments and suggestions without the insults.<P>Bruce <BR>88GT </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I agree with your post. Fuel containers should be lined, but aren't due to cost and corporate influence. When I was young, decades ago, my dad was innovative and would take the bodies off of cars, shorten the frames considerably, and make dune buggies out of them. Well, those were the good 'ole days when society was a little more Neanderthal and patriarchal (now I'll get hate mail). If a mishap were to occur, it would be just that; a mishap. Now it's gross negligence or negligent homicide. Before you guys modify too much, think about the possible repercussions. With all the vehicle testing in the media, what would a prosecutor and/or jury think about taking a perfectly good, tested structure and modifying it for the sake of vanity, and causing serious injury or death? I'm not trying to be the Fiero club grim reaper, but think about the potential repercussions. When you go to buy your fuel cell/bladder, what will you be required to sign? Could it be a disclaimer stating you must never use your new purchase for anything except off-road stuff? Yep. Doing a rebody on a vehicle is one thing, dropping in a different engine is a little more extreme, but altering the fuel container is the most critical endeavor/risk you might ever take on. A mutual risk understanding exists between motorists on the road. That understanding is that we will all behave predictably. If something is not tested, it's not predictable. The same attitude exists with aircraft, and they undergo the same scrutiny and might be limited or eliminated one day. Make sure it's worth it and remember that you can never comfortably sell your car....EVER. An example of this is when a person builds an aircraft from kit. That person becomes the manufacturer of an experimentally licensed aircraft. If that person has a lot of money, he or she will destroy the aircraft as opposed to selling it for $30k-$60k or more. Could you imagine selling your car for 3 or 4k one day and having to live with that liability forever? You would have to deregister it and sell it for off-road use only. Of course you would receive considerably less for it. And that's if no mishap occurred while you owned it. I'm not trying to dampen the spirit, just inform people who might not want to hear the truth. I would never modify the fuel cell.<P>You wrote:<P>"It is possible to modify and customize a vehicle without compromising safety."<P>Unless you do elaborate testing, that is nothing more than an unsubstantiated opinion. In 1965, you would be right. Today, tell that to a jury with the information that the Ford Pinto and Chevy trucks (1973-1987) provided us. You could argue that Fieros had inherent fuel problems from the factory in that they tended to ignite while driving down the road. Well, that was fixed through the installation of the heat shield. How about your auto liability insurance policy. Does it void your policy if you make considerable modifications to the design or operating limitations of your vehicle without disclosure, hence higher premiums? I've seen some policies that double the premiums with wider tires and competition wheels. When I was 16 I stole and totalled my parents car. The insurance company paid off when they didn't have to in order to act in good faith. Now, insurance companies look for every loop-hole to crawl through to avoid paying. All I'm saying is weigh the costs vs. the advantages before you do anything drastic. Who knows, maybe engine changes will be considered as drastic as a fuel cell relocation one day and we will all be relegated to hanging different color air fresheners from our mirrors to pronounce our individuality. Not to tangent, but this was the type of concern Ted Kozinski (Unibomber) had. Now who's crazy?||||reg||1
Z||000016||EdsB52||06-05-2001||02:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>I was poking around under my car last weekend (and the weekend before, and...).<BR>There seems to be a void in the chassis directly in front of the gas tank. If you cut into that space, It might be useable for another 1/2 - 1 gallon of fuel.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Exactly, that viod, I would guess, is probably there to allow the subframe to colapse in the event of an impact. I know your just jokin' around. I cannot stress enough that I would never mess with the fuel tank or surrounding structure unless I was going to permanently designate that car to off-road use.||||reg||1
